export const AI_CONTEXT = "You are an expert on Salesforce Salesforce OmniStudio (OMNISTUDIO) Spring '26.\nAnswer questions based ONLY on the following product knowledge. If the question is not about OMNISTUDIO or you are unsure, say so clearly. Keep answers concise (2-4 sentences).\nWhen mentioning domains or components, include markdown links using the URLs from the Links section below.\n\n## Domains (12)\n- **OmniScripts** (omniscripts): OmniStudio Omniscripts are declarative, guided interactions that display information, collect and validate user input, perform calculations, and create or update data.\n- **FlexCards** (flexcards): Flexcards are OmniStudio’s declarative UI components for displaying contextual information and launching relevant actions on the Salesforce platform.\n- **Integration Procedures** (integration): Integration Procedures are OmniStudio server-side processes that combine data operations, transformations, callouts, business rules, and supporting actions into reusable multistep transactions without an end-user UI.\n- **Data Mappers** (datamappers): Data Mappers are OmniStudio's declarative, server-side components for reading or writing Salesforce object data and performing single-step data-structure transformations.\n- **Functions & Formulas** (formulas): Functions & Formulas provides expression capabilities for calculations, transformations, comparisons, and conditional evaluation across OmniStudio.\n- **Custom LWC Extensions** (lwc): Custom LWC Extensions provide the developer extension point for adding specialized Lightning Web Component behavior to OmniStudio user experiences.\n- **Configuration & Settings** (config): Configuration & Settings centralizes org-level administration for OmniStudio runtime behavior, Integration Procedure execution, security enforcement, caching, and Experience Cloud usage.\n- **Experience Cloud** (experience): Experience Cloud extends OmniStudio guided processes and contextual interfaces to customer, partner, and public-facing sites.\n- **Agentforce Integration** (agentforce): Agentforce Integration connects OmniStudio components with Agentforce agents, subagents, and actions.\n- **OmniStudio Analytics** (analytics): OmniStudio Analytics uses OmniAnalytics to track user interactions with Omniscripts and Flexcards on the OmniStudio standard runtime and standard objects.\n- **Testing & Quality** (testing): Testing & Quality covers OmniStudio tools for validating Omniscript and Flexcard configurations during design and before deployment.\n- **Deployment & Migration** (deployment): Deployment & Migration covers packaging, versioning, and moving OmniStudio components across Salesforce environments.\n\n## Key Components (51)\n- **OmniScript Designer** [OmniScripts]: OmniScript Designer is the declarative workspace for creating and managing guided OmniStudio processes. Create an Omniscript from the Omniscripts list view by entering its Name, Type, SubType, and Language. Type and SubType must be alphanumeric and cannot contain spaces or underscores, and their combination with Language identifies the compiled Omniscript Lightning web component. In the designer, drag elements to the canvas and edit their properties while building the guided process. Setup options apply to the entire Omniscript and include reusable behavior, step chart placement, save options, logging metrics, messaging, and styling.\n- **Input Elements** [OmniScripts]: Input Elements collect or present values for users to enter, select, upload, or change in an Omniscript. Add an input element inside a Step or another supported group and configure its Name, Field Label, default value, validation, and element-specific properties. Select, Multi-Select, and Radio elements can obtain options manually, from a Salesforce object, or from a custom Apex class and method. Date, Date/Time, and Time elements support display formats and selectable ranges, while Number, Currency, Telephone, Password, and Text elements provide controls for allowed values or formats. Checkbox values are boolean and return true or false to the Data JSON.\n- **Display & Formula Elements** [OmniScripts]: Display & Formula Elements present formatted information and calculate or evaluate values within an Omniscript. Use a Text Block to add text, images, merge fields, and other supported rich content through the configured rich text editor. A Line Break forces following elements onto a new line and can add padding below the break. OmniStudio uses TinyMCE by default and can use Lightning Rich Text Editor when the EnableLightningRTE Omni Interaction Configuration is enabled. Tables aren't supported inside Omniscript Text Block elements.\n- **Action Elements** [OmniScripts]: Action Elements execute data operations, invoke services, communicate with other components, and direct users to other experiences. Use Omnistudio Data Mapper Extract and Turbo Actions to retrieve Salesforce data, a Data Mapper Post Action to create or update Salesforce records, and a Data Mapper Transform Action to restructure or convert data. An Integration Procedure Action invokes a server-side Integration Procedure that can combine multiple operations and data sources. HTTP Actions call supported web services, while Remote Actions call Apex classes and methods that implement the documented interfaces. Input parameters and merge fields pass Omniscript data into these actions, and response paths and nodes can trim or reparent returned JSON.\n- **Step & Group Elements** [OmniScripts]: Step & Group Elements organize an Omniscript into pages, logical data structures, repeatable entries, questionnaires, and parallel action collections. Each Step represents a page in the Omniscript and can contain any element except another Step or a child Omniscript. Step properties control labels, instructions, navigation button presentation, save-for-later availability, Knowledge options, messaging, and conditional display. Required fields and validation are configured on the elements inside the Step, and a validation failure prevents navigation to the next Step. The Step Chart can be hidden or positioned through Omniscript setup options.\n- **Saved Sessions & Resume** [OmniScripts]: Saved Sessions & Resume lets users save an in-progress Omniscript and return to it later. Enable Allow save for later in the Omniscript Setup panel and optionally allow or disable saving on individual Steps. Configure a Save Name Template, an expiration period, and the object ID used to associate the saved Omniscript with a record. Users can save manually, or the Omniscript can save automatically when they select Next. After a saved session expires, the Omniscript restarts without loading the saved data.\n- **Activation & Compilation** [OmniScripts]: Activation & Compilation makes a completed Omniscript available for runtime use. Before activation, ensure that embedded components are active and verify the Omniscript in Preview. In the standard runtime, add an activated Omniscript to Lightning App Builder or Experience Builder by using the standard Omniscript component and selecting its Type, SubType, Language, theme, and display options. Aura-based and Lightning Web Runtime Experience Cloud sites are supported with the documented component and wrapper configurations. A custom Lightning web component can reference the Omniscript through the omnistudio standard runtime wrapper by supplying the required Type, SubType, and Language.\n- **FlexCard Designer** [FlexCards]: The FlexCard Designer is OmniStudio’s declarative environment for creating contextual UI components without code. The FlexCard Designer provides a drag-based canvas for assembling customer-centric interfaces from configurable elements. In the OmniStudio standard designer, designers can drag elements to the canvas and edit their properties at the same time without switching between tabs. The designer supports creating, managing, previewing, and optimizing Flexcards through a declarative workflow focused on business logic rather than programming.\n- **Data Sources** [FlexCards]: Data Sources connect Flexcards to contextual information from Salesforce and external systems. Configure a data source to retrieve the information displayed by a Flexcard. Flexcards can pull data from multiple sources and use the returned values in their fields, elements, conditions, and actions. Context variables such as recordId, Parent, Session, User, record, or action provide runtime values that can be passed to data sources or referenced by card configurations.\n- **Card States & Conditions** [FlexCards]: Card States & Conditions control which Flexcard presentation is shown for the current data and context. Define states to vary a Flexcard’s layout and available interactions according to data conditions. Each state can present a different combination of fields, elements, and actions for the current record or returned data. This lets one card respond dynamically to context while remaining focused on displaying information and supporting simple interactions.\n- **Actions & Flyouts** [FlexCards]: Actions & Flyouts make Flexcards interactive while keeping information within the customer’s current context. Configure actions on a Flexcard to connect displayed information with the next business task. Supported uses include launching or updating an Omniscript, navigating to another page or application, firing an event, updating field values, and displaying a flyout. For example, an account card can provide actions to close a case, open a case, or create a task.\n- **Child Cards & Embedding** [FlexCards]: Child Cards & Embedding support modular Flexcard compositions and placement within broader OmniStudio experiences. Use child Flexcards to divide a larger interface into reusable card units. Create and test child Flexcards before configuring the parent, because a child Flexcard must be active before the parent can reference it. The parent’s configuration retains the child-card references used by the composition.\n- **IP Designer** [Integration Procedures]: IP Designer is the declarative environment for building server-side Integration Procedures in OmniStudio. An Integration Procedure bundles server-side data integration operations for efficiency and reuse. Use it when a process must read from and write to Salesforce, work with unrelated objects, combine multiple data-source types, or perform transformations that require more than direct mappings and formulas. Integration Procedures can also send email, merge lists, and handle errors.\n- **Data Actions** [Integration Procedures]: Data Actions connect an Integration Procedure to reusable OmniStudio Data Mapper operations and other data-processing steps. Use Data Mapper actions to incorporate declarative Salesforce data access and transformation into an Integration Procedure. Multiple Data Mappers can be called when a process must both read and write records or work with Salesforce objects that don't have a defined relationship. A single Integration Procedure can combine these operations with REST, Apex, external-object, or other supported data sources.\n- **HTTP & Remote Actions** [Integration Procedures]: HTTP & Remote Actions let Integration Procedures exchange data with REST endpoints and invoke Apex implementations. Use HTTP actions when an Integration Procedure must read from or write to a REST API as part of a multistep integration. Returned data can be processed by later actions, transformations, conditions, or response logic. Because Integration Procedures run without an end-user UI, they can centralize these callouts for reuse by multiple calling components.\n- **Flow Control Blocks** [Integration Procedures]: Flow Control Blocks organize Integration Procedure actions and determine how groups of actions execute. Use an If-Else Conditional Block to define mutually exclusive execution paths. Conditions can use formula expressions and values available to the Integration Procedure, allowing different action groups to run for different data or business circumstances. This is useful when transformations or calculations must occur only under selected conditions.\n- **Decision & Intelligence Actions** [Integration Procedures]: Decision & Intelligence Actions incorporate rules-based evaluation into an Integration Procedure. Use decision actions when business rules must be evaluated as part of a server-side orchestration flow. An Integration Procedure can invoke Decision Matrices and Expression Sets, then pass their results to later data operations, branches, or response processing. Centralizing the evaluation in an Integration Procedure makes the same backend logic reusable by different callers.\n- **Utility Actions** [Integration Procedures]: Utility Actions perform supporting server-side tasks within an Integration Procedure. Use a Response Action to return data to the entity that called the Integration Procedure. The response can expose the data selected for the caller after preceding actions, transformations, and conditions finish. Integration Procedures can be invoked from Omniscripts, Flexcards, Apex, REST, Flow, and scheduled jobs.\n- **IP Activation & Caching** [Integration Procedures]: IP Activation & Caching prepares an Integration Procedure version for runtime use and improves repeated execution paths. Create and validate a new component version before replacing an active Integration Procedure, particularly when changing designers or testing in another org. The standard designer supports activation after edits are saved and validated. Activated Integration Procedures can then provide backend logic to Omniscripts or act as data sources for Flexcards.\n- **Extract (Read)** [Data Mappers]: Extract (Read) retrieves mapped data from one or more related Salesforce objects and returns a structured response. A Data Mapper Extract reads Salesforce object data without requiring a custom data-access implementation. It can retrieve fields from related objects and map source values to output nodes. A single Data Mapper is appropriate when the objects have defined relationships and the operation only reads, rather than both reading and writing, Salesforce data.\n- **Load (Write)** [Data Mappers]: Load (Write) creates or updates Salesforce records from mapped input data. A Data Mapper Load writes data to Salesforce objects. Use a single Data Mapper when the operation writes, but does not also read, Salesforce data and the affected objects have defined relationships. If a process must both read and write records, an Integration Procedure can orchestrate separate Data Mappers.\n- **Transform (JSON-to-JSON)** [Data Mappers]: Transform (JSON-to-JSON) changes an in-memory data structure without directly reading from or writing to Salesforce objects. A Data Mapper Transform works with JSON, XML, or other supported in-memory input rather than performing a Salesforce database operation. It maps input nodes to output nodes and can change the shape or names of the resulting data. This makes it suitable for single-step conversion and restructuring tasks.\n- **Turbo Extract** [Data Mappers]: Turbo Extract is a performance-oriented Data Mapper for reading fields from one Salesforce object and related parent objects. A Data Mapper Turbo Extract retrieves fields from a primary Salesforce object and related parent objects. Use it when direct, high-performance extraction is more appropriate than the formulas and configurable output mappings available with a standard Extract. The operation reads data and does not write Salesforce records.\n- **Mapping Formulas** [Data Mappers]: Mapping Formulas apply supported formula expressions while a Data Mapper filters, calculates, converts, or reformats values. Data Mapper mappings can use OmniStudio formula expressions to perform supported operations on data values. Formulas can provide filtering, calculations, and reformatting within a declarative mapping. This allows a single Data Mapper to handle processing that does not require separate orchestration steps.\n- **Math & Logical Functions** [Functions & Formulas]: Math & Logical Functions provide calculations, comparisons, and conditional evaluation within OmniStudio formulas. Conditional expressions use functions such as IF to choose between results based on an evaluated condition. An IF condition cannot evaluate an array that contains an empty string, including an array populated from an empty field. Formula authors must therefore ensure that the condition receives an acceptable value before evaluation.\n- **String Functions** [Functions & Formulas]: String Functions operate on text values in OmniStudio formulas. The documented string functions include BASE64ENCODE(data), CONCAT(string...), JOIN(string..., token), MAXSTRING(string...), SPLIT(string, token), STRINGINDEXOF(string, substring), SUBSTRING(string, startIndex, endIndex), and TOSTRING(data). JOIN combines string values using a specified token, while SPLIT separates a string at that token. Both functions require a correctly formed token parameter.\n- **Date & Time Functions** [Functions & Formulas]: Date & Time Functions extract date or time portions, calculate differences, and provide current-date values in OmniStudio formulas. Date and time extraction functions return a selected part of an input value. For example, YEAR applied to a CreatedDate value can return its four-digit year. DAY, HOUR, MINUTE, and SECOND provide corresponding date or time portions, while TIMEDIFF compares two time values.\n- **List & JSON Functions** [Functions & Formulas]: List & JSON Functions work with grouped values, arrays, and JSON structures in OmniStudio formulas. A list is a comma-separated series of values that is often enclosed in parentheses. Its values can have different data types and can include nested lists, and the list itself is anonymous. Examples include integer lists such as (1, 2, 3) and string lists such as (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\").\n- **Data Types & Conversion** [Functions & Formulas]: Data Types & Conversion defines how values are represented and supplied to OmniStudio formulas. Strings are sequences of characters that can represent words, numbers, or dates. They must be enclosed in matching single or double quotation marks, and matching quote types can be nested within the other type. Strings are sorted lexicographically by comparing their individual characters.\n- **Custom OmniScript Elements** [Custom LWC Extensions]: Custom OmniScript Elements extend the OmniStudio Omniscript element palette when declarative elements don’t meet a guided-process requirement. OmniStudio uses a Lightning Web Component runtime, and an activated or generated Omniscript can run as a Lightning Web Component. Custom elements provide a developer extension point beyond the elements available in the declarative Omniscript designer. They participate in guided interactions that display information, collect input, and respond to user choices.\n- **Custom FlexCard Components** [Custom LWC Extensions]: Custom FlexCard Components extend Flexcard rendering beyond the elements available in the declarative designer. A Flexcard is a contextual block that combines pertinent information with links or actions, and the generated Flexcard is a Lightning Web Component. Custom Lightning Web Components extend how a Flexcard renders content when its standard elements aren’t sufficient. The resulting component remains part of a Flexcard experience deployed to Lightning or Experience Cloud pages.\n- **Data Context & Events** [Custom LWC Extensions]: Data Context & Events connects custom Lightning Web Components with the runtime context of an Omniscript or Flexcard. An Omniscript carries values and responses through its runtime data structure as users progress through the guided interaction. A custom Lightning Web Component can use the Omniscript data context and documented interfaces to work with that data rather than maintaining a separate process model. Event integration allows the component to participate in Omniscript behavior such as navigation and validation.\n- **Interaction Configuration** [Configuration & Settings]: Interaction Configuration uses Omni Interaction Configuration records to control org-level OmniStudio behavior through named settings and values. Omni Interaction Configuration records provide org-level settings for OmniStudio features. Administrators create a record from Omni Interaction Configuration in Setup, enter the documented setting name in Label, and enter its setting value in Value. Named configurations include RetainDesignerSettingOnUpgrade, UserLocaleDateTime, EnableLightningRTE, SkipUserProfileOnOSLoad, and ErrorLoggingEnabled. The effect of each record depends on the exact setting named by the documentation.\n- **Global Auto Number** [Configuration & Settings]: Global Auto Number provides configurable unique numbers for use cases such as sales orders, cases, and loan applications. Create an Omni Global Auto Number record after turning on the Omni Global Auto Number OmniStudio Setting and completing the documented prerequisites. Its Name is the unique identifier passed to the GLOBALAUTONUMBER function. Increment controls how much the number increases, and Last Generated Number provides the starting value and is updated whenever the function executes. Left Pad and Minimum Length control padding, while Prefix and Separator optionally format the generated value.\n- **Usage-Based Entitlements** [Configuration & Settings]: Usage-Based Entitlements show how many OmniStudio calls an org is entitled to use and how many have been consumed. An OmniStudio call is the primary unit of usage consumption, and tracking occurs at the org level. Usage resets monthly according to the organization's contract terms. A parent Omniscript invocation counts as one call, regardless of child Omniscripts invoked within that parent. Child Omniscript executions aren't recorded as additional calls.\n- **Electronic Signatures** [Configuration & Settings]: Electronic Signatures integrate DocuSign documents with OmniStudio guided processes. Configure the connection by creating a DocuSign application that uses Authorization Code Grant, an authentication provider, and an OAuth 2.0 named credential. Omni Interaction Configuration records named DocuSignAccountId and DocuSignNamedCredential provide the DocuSign API Account ID and named credential name. For Experience Cloud sites, add the DocuSign login page as a trusted URL and allow frame-src content because DocuSign runs in an iframe. A Send on Behalf named credential can also be associated with users when documents must be sent or signed on behalf of another user.\n- **Experience Builder Components** [Experience Cloud]: Experience Builder Components bring OmniStudio guided interactions and contextual interfaces to Experience Cloud sites. In the standard runtime, add an active Omniscript to an Experience Builder page by dragging the Omniscript component onto the canvas. Aura-based sites list the component under Process Automation, while LWR Experience Builder lists it under Customer Interactions. Select the required type and subtype, theme, display mode, language, and language direction, and then preview the site. An Omniscript can appear directly on the page or open from a button.\n- **Guest & Authenticated Access** [Experience Cloud]: Guest & Authenticated Access controls which Experience Cloud users can read and run public-facing OmniStudio content while preserving object- and field-level security. Experience Cloud users require access to the OmniStudio content used by public-facing components. For authenticated users, create a permission set that grants Read and View All Fields access to the documented Omni Process, Omni Process Compilation, and Omni Process Element records, and use an appropriate Experience Cloud user profile. Partner Experience Cloud users also require the documented field-level access for Omni Process Compilation, Omni Data Transformation, and Omniscript Saved Sessions in the standard runtime. Sharing rules can provide access to the necessary component records for testing or partner-facing use cases.\n- **Custom LWC Extensions** [Experience Cloud]: Custom LWC Extensions adapt or embed generated OmniStudio Lightning Web Components for Experience Cloud use cases. An active Omniscript can be referenced from a custom Lightning Web Component through the standard runtime wrapper. The wrapper identifies the Omniscript by mandatory type, subtype, and language attributes, and it uses the Lightning theme by default unless the Newport theme is specified. For an LWR Experience Cloud site, a wrapper component is required when an Omniscript is referenced from another Lightning Web Component. Generated Flexcards and Omniscripts are also Lightning Web Components that can be modified based on business logic.\n- **Agent Actions** [Agentforce Integration]: Agent Actions connect Agentforce agents to executable OmniStudio components. An Integration Procedure must be prepared before it can be used as an Agent Action because Agentforce expects a different data format from the standard Integration Procedure format. In the Integration Procedure preview, select Configure Agentforce and define the input and output data in JSON. JSON keys can contain only alphanumeric characters and cannot contain special characters. Verifying the data automatically updates the structure to a format compatible with Agentforce.\n- **Context Surfaces** [Agentforce Integration]: Context Surfaces use Flexcards to provide relevant OmniStudio context for agents. Flexcards serve as context surfaces for agents, presenting relevant information within agent-assisted interactions. They complement executable OmniScripts and Integration Procedures by supplying a contextual interface rather than performing the server-side action itself. Flexcards can also invoke Integration Procedures, consistent with their role in connected OmniStudio experiences.\n- **Agent Orchestration** [Agentforce Integration]: Agent Orchestration coordinates agents, subagents, Agent Actions, and OmniStudio components around specific conversation intents and tasks. In Agentforce Studio, an agent manages the conversation flow and executes Agent Actions. Subagents define the particular intents or tasks that the agent handles and are associated with the relevant actions. An Integration Procedure action is added to a selected subagent from the Asset Library after its data has been prepared and the Integration Procedure has been activated. This organization enables the agent to invoke the configured automation during customer interactions.\n- **Tracking Definitions** [OmniStudio Analytics]: Tracking Definitions configure which active Omniscripts and Flexcards OmniAnalytics tracks and where their interaction data is sent. Enable OmniAnalytics from Setup by turning on Omnistudio Analytics. Enable Event Notifications publishes tracking data to OmniTrackingEvent standard platform events, while Enable Event Internal Writes logs tracking data in Decision Explainer Service stores. Either storage option, or both, can be enabled according to the required data flow.\n- **Engagement Metrics** [OmniStudio Analytics]: Engagement Metrics measure user activity in tracked Omniscripts and Flexcards. OmniAnalytics collects clicks per product, offer, or promotion impression and clicks per UI action impression. It also measures the percentage of Omniscripts completed and the percentage of Omniscript steps completed. These measurements support analysis of purchasing and other guided processes from initial interaction through completion.\n- **Analytics Dashboards** [OmniStudio Analytics]: Analytics Dashboards present tracked OmniStudio interaction data for individual Omniscripts and Flexcards. After tracked components are added to an activated Lightning App Page, open the applicable Tracking Group and run a report from an Omniscript or Flexcard tab. Omniscript reports show overall information and allow users to select the eye icon for details about a step. Analytics report data is updated at 30-minute intervals. Missing Omniscript steps can indicate that Step Label values or required Messaging Framework settings aren't configured.\n- **UTAM Test Framework** [Testing & Quality]: The UTAM Test Framework supports automated end-to-end UI testing for OmniStudio Omniscripts and Flexcards. UTAM provides a structured approach to automating OmniStudio user-interface tests. Test authors interact with Page Object Models instead of directly coupling every test step to the rendered interface. This abstraction supports end-to-end browser testing of Omniscripts and Flexcards.\n- **Design Assistant** [Testing & Quality]: OmniStudio Design Assistant provides real-time feedback about the health and design quality of Omniscripts and Flexcards while they are being authored. OmniStudio Design Assistant monitors Omniscript and Flexcard designs and provides actionable alerts within their designers. It assesses complexity by considering elements, data sources, actions, and other design factors. The resulting feedback helps identify design inefficiencies that can contribute to latency, low throughput, resource overuse, or potential runtime failures.\n- **Preview & Debug** [Testing & Quality]: Preview & Debug capabilities let designers exercise OmniStudio components during development before activation or publication. Preview an Omniscript to run through its guided process and verify how the component displays, collects, and responds to data. Inspect the runtime Data JSON as the interaction progresses to understand element values and action responses. Exercise different inputs to validate conditional logic and alternate branches before activating the Omniscript.\n- **DataPack Export/Import** [Deployment & Migration]: DataPack Export/Import supports moving collections of OmniStudio components and related functionality between Salesforce environments. An Omni DataPack is a collection of OmniStudio components and related functionality packaged for deployment between orgs. A DataPack can contain Omniscript, Flexcard, Integration Procedure, and Data Mapper definitions. This packaging supports a development lifecycle in which solutions are created in a development org, tested in a test or integration org, and then deployed to production.\n- **Version Management** [Deployment & Migration]: Version Management supports creating editable versions of OmniStudio content while preserving the Salesforce-provided base version. Version 1 of standard OmniStudio content is never editable, and Salesforce pushes release updates to that version. To modify a standard component, create a separate version in the designer. The created version remains independent and doesn't update when later releases modify Version 1.\n- **Dependency Resolution** [Deployment & Migration]: Dependency Resolution addresses the related OmniStudio components and Salesforce metadata that must be considered during deployment planning. Select a deployment tool according to what is being deployed and the data model and OmniStudio runtime used by the source and target orgs. Salesforce CLI supports Omniscripts, Flexcards, Integration Procedures, Data Mappers, other Salesforce components, and metadata such as FlexiPage, PermissionSet, and OmniStudio Lightning Web Components. Its compatibility is limited to standard objects in the source org and standard objects with standard runtime in the target org.\n\n## Key Objects (12)\n### OmniScripts\n- **OmniProcess**: OmniProcess stores the definition and version-level configuration for Omniscripts and Integration Procedures.\n- **OmniProcessElement**: OmniProcessElement stores individual Omniscript elements associated with an OmniProcess.\n- **OmniProcessCompilation**: OmniProcessCompilation stores compiled Omniscripts and Integration Procedures.\n- **OmniProcessTransientData**: OmniProcessTransientData stores temporary data for long-running Omniscript and Integration Procedure processes.\n- **OmniscriptSavedSession**: OmniscriptSavedSession stores saved Omniscript sessions used by save-and-resume functionality.\n### FlexCards\n- **OmniUiCard**: OmniUiCard stores the definition and versioned configuration of a Flexcard, including its Name, Author, Description, IsActive status, and VersionNumber.\n### Data Mappers\n- **OmniDataTransformation**: OmniDataTransformation stores the main definition of an OmniStudio Data Mapper, including its Name, InterfaceName, Type, activation status, description, and override key.\n- **OmniDataTransformationItem**: OmniDataTransformationItem stores Data Mapper mapping metadata for input and output objects and fields.\n### Configuration & Settings\n- **OmniElectronicSignatureTemplate**: OmniElectronicSignatureTemplate represents a DocuSign signature template used by Omniscripts.\n- **OmniInteractionConfig**: OmniInteractionConfig represents a named OmniStudio configuration entry that controls documented org-level behavior.\n### OmniStudio Analytics\n- **OmniAnalyticsTrackingDef**: OmniAnalyticsTrackingDef stores an OmniAnalytics tracking definition and identifies its TrackingGroup, EventType, and ComponentType.\n### Deployment & Migration\n- **OmniDataPack**: OmniDataPack represents a collection of OmniStudio components and related functionality packaged to deploy objects from one sandbox or development org to another org, such as production.\n\n## Object Fields\n### OmniProcess (13 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name identifying this OmniScript or Integration Procedure. For OmniScripts, the name is separate from the Type/SubType/Language unique key\n- Type (Text): Primary classification grouping related processes together. For OmniScripts, must start with a letter and contain only alphanumeric characters without spaces or underscores. For Integration Procedures, can contain letters, numbers, and special characters but no spaces\n- SubType (Text): Secondary classification within the type. Combined with Type (and Language for OmniScripts), creates the unique identifier. Must be alphanumeric with no spaces or underscores for OmniScripts. Combined Type, SubType, and Language must not exceed 60 characters\n- Language (Text): Language code for the process definition. Standard values include English and Multi-Language. Multi-Language enables a single OmniScript to render in multiple languages using custom label translations. Requires Translation Workbench to be enabled\n- VersionNumber (Number): Numeric version identifier allowing multiple versions of the same process. Increments automatically when creating new versions. Only one version per Type/SubType/Language combination can be active at a time\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this version is the active runtime version for its Type/SubType/Language combination. Setting to true triggers compilation and deactivates any previously active version. Must not be set to true during import operations\n- IsIntegrationProcedure (Checkbox): When true, this record is an Integration Procedure (server-side orchestration with no UI). When false, it is an OmniScript (guided user interface). Both types share the same object but have different designer experiences and capabilities\n- IsWebCompEnabled (Checkbox): Indicates the process is compiled as a Lightning Web Component for LWC runtime. On standard runtime with Managed Package Runtime disabled, this is the default compilation mode\n- RequiredPermission (Text): Custom permission API name required for a user to execute this process. When set, only users with this custom permission assigned through their profile or permission set can run the OmniScript or Integration Procedure\n- ResponseCacheType (Picklist): Caching strategy for Integration Procedure responses. Values include None (no caching), Session (user-specific cache, max 8 hours), and Org (shared cache, max 48 hours). Applies only to Integration Procedures. Minimum cache TTL is 5 minutes\n- PropertySetConfig (LongTextArea): JSON configuration blob storing all process-level settings including element definitions, styling options, navigation labels, save behavior, conditional logic, and custom properties. Creating a new version triggers a class that adds any missing JSON properties to this field\n- Description (TextArea): Human-readable description of the process purpose and behavior. Optional but recommended for documentation and searchability in the list view\n- CustomJavaScript (LongTextArea): Custom JavaScript code injected into the OmniScript runtime for advanced logic. Used for scenarios where declarative configuration is insufficient. Applies to OmniScripts only, not Integration Procedures\n### OmniProcessElement (8 fields)\n- Name (Text): Unique element name used for data binding and merge field references within the process. The name becomes the JSON key in the OmniScript data model. Must be unique within its parent scope\n- Type (Picklist): Element type determining its behavior. Values include Step, Text, Number, Currency, Date, Time, Email, URL, Telephone, Password, TextArea, Select, Multi-Select, Lookup, Checkbox, Radio, Range, File, Image, Signature, Disclosure, Formula, Aggregate, Messaging, Text Block, DataRaptor Extract Action, DataRaptor Post Action, DataRaptor Transform Action, Integration Procedure Action, Remote Action, HTTP Action, Email Action, DocuSign Envelope Action, DocuSign Signature Action, Navigate Action, Set Values, Set Errors, Edit Block, Action Block, Custom LWC, and OmniScript\n- SequenceNumber (Number): Determines the order of this element within its parent step or block. Elements are processed sequentially by this number. Reordering in the designer updates these values automatically\n- Level (Number): Nesting depth of the element in the process hierarchy. Level 0 for top-level steps and actions placed directly on the canvas. Level 1 for elements within steps. Level 2 for elements nested within blocks inside steps. Increases with each nesting layer\n- Description (TextArea): Optional description of the element purpose for documentation. Visible to designers but not displayed to end users. Useful for explaining complex conditional logic or action configuration to other administrators\n- PropertySetConfig (LongTextArea): JSON configuration containing all element-specific settings including data source references, validation rules, conditional visibility expressions, default values, label text, help text, required status, error messages, styling options, and action parameters. The structure varies by element type\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this element is included in the active process version. Inactive elements are preserved in the definition but excluded from compilation and runtime execution\n- EmbeddedOmniScriptKey (Text): Type/SubType/Language key referencing another OmniScript embedded within this element, for example accountCreateEnglish. Used when the element type is OmniScript to specify which child OmniScript to embed. The referenced OmniScript must be activated separately\n### OmniProcessCompilation (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Auto-generated name identifying the compilation record, typically derived from the parent OmniProcess Type and SubType\n- OmniProcessId (Lookup): Reference to the parent OmniProcess this compilation was generated from. Each activation creates a new compilation record linked to the specific version\n- Status (Picklist): Compilation lifecycle status. Active indicates this is the current runtime version. Superseded means a newer compilation replaced it. Error indicates the activation failed due to invalid element references or configuration issues\n- CompilationData (LongTextArea): The full compiled JSON payload containing all pre-processed element configurations, resolved merge fields, and validated data bindings. This payload is loaded directly by the runtime engine without additional processing\n- CompiledDate (DateTime): Timestamp when this compilation was generated. Useful for auditing when versions were activated and tracking deployment timelines\n### OmniProcessTransientData (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Identifier for this transient data record\n- OmniProcessId (Lookup): The OmniProcess this transient data is associated with\n- DataPayload (LongTextArea): JSON blob containing the intermediate process data\n- ExpirationDate (DateTime): Date and time after which this transient data is eligible for cleanup\n- Status (Picklist): Current state of the transient data such as Active, Consumed, or Expired\n- InstanceId (Text): Unique execution instance identifier linking this data to a specific process run\n### OmniscriptSavedSession (8 fields)\n- Name (Text): Auto-generated name for the saved session record\n- OmniProcessId (Lookup): The OmniScript process this saved session belongs to\n- DataPayload (LongTextArea): Serialized JSON of all form inputs and data collected up to the save point\n- UserId (Lookup): The user who saved this session\n- Status (Picklist): Session state such as In Progress, Resumed, or Completed\n- LastSavedPage (Text): Step name or page index where the user left off\n- CreatedDate (DateTime): Timestamp when the session was first saved\n- ExpirationDate (DateTime): Date after which this saved session is no longer resumable\n### OmniUiCard (9 fields)\n- Name (Text): Unique name identifying this FlexCard definition. Combined with Author to form the unique identifier. Can only contain letters, numbers, and underscores. Must begin with a letter, cannot end with an underscore or contain two consecutive underscores. Cannot use reserved words\n- Author (Text): Name of the admin or developer who created this FlexCard. Combined with Name to form the unique identifier in the org. Follows the same character restrictions as Name\n- Description (TextArea): Human-readable description of the card purpose and the data it displays. Visible in the list view and designer for documentation purposes\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this FlexCard version is available for use in Lightning pages, Experience Cloud sites, and OmniScript embeds. Only one version per Name/Author combination can be active at a time\n- VersionNumber (Number): Numeric version allowing multiple iterations of the same card definition. Recommended limit is 10 versions per FlexCard. Only the last modified version appears in the list view\n- PropertySetConfig (LongTextArea): JSON configuration storing the complete card definition including element layout (using a 12-column grid), styling rules, conditional visibility expressions, state definitions with their conditions, action configurations (OmniScript launch, navigation, flyout, set values, events), and responsive breakpoints for different device sizes\n- DataSourceConfig (LongTextArea): JSON defining the data sources that feed the card. Supported types include Integration Procedures (recommended for production), SOQL queries (for simple single-object scenarios), Apex class methods, REST API endpoints, and Streaming API sources (runtime only, not in standard designer). Each source maps response fields to card elements through data binding\n- ChildCardKeys (Text): Comma-separated keys referencing child FlexCards embedded within this parent card. Recommended limit is 7 child cards per parent. Each child receives the parent's data context through the Parent.attr variable. Supports 1 recursive FlexCard maximum\n- SampleDataSourceResponse (LongTextArea): Sample JSON response used in the FlexCard designer preview mode. Allows designers to preview the card layout with representative data without connecting to live data sources. Useful during initial design before Integration Procedures are built\n### OmniDataTransformation (9 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name for this Data Mapper definition. Separate from the InterfaceName API identifier\n- InterfaceName (Text): Unique API identifier used to invoke this Data Mapper from Integration Procedures, OmniScripts, Apex, and REST APIs. Must be unique across the org. Can contain letters, numbers, and dashes but no spaces. This is the name referenced in action element configurations\n- Type (Picklist): Operation type determining behavior. Extract reads data via SOQL queries. Load writes data via DML insert or upsert operations. Transform reshapes JSON structures without database access (also supports DocuSign output type). Turbo Extract uses an optimized read path for high-volume data retrieval\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this Data Mapper version is available for execution. Only one version per InterfaceName can be active at a time. Activation makes this version the live one referenced by calling components\n- Description (TextArea): Human-readable explanation of what data this mapper reads, writes, or transforms. Visible in the list view for documentation and searchability\n- OverrideKey (Text): Key allowing runtime selection between multiple implementations of the same interface. Enables A/B testing or environment-specific Data Mapper versions without changing the calling component configuration\n- PreviewOtherData (LongTextArea): Sample input JSON data used for previewing the transformation in the designer. Allows testing field mappings and formula expressions without connecting to live data sources or running actual queries\n- TargetOutputDocumentIdentifier (Text): DocuSign template ID for Transform Data Mappers with DocuSign output type. When set, the mapper fetches the template's field definitions and enables mapping from input JSON to DocuSign custom fields (Text, Radio Button, Checkbox tabs)\n- SynchronousProcessThreshold (Number): Record count threshold above which a Load Data Mapper switches to asynchronous batch Apex processing. When the bulkUpload parameter is true, records exceeding this threshold are processed in batch mode to avoid governor limits on large data loads\n### OmniDataTransformationItem (11 fields)\n- Name (Text): Name identifying this mapping item within the Data Mapper\n- InputObjectName (Text): Source object API name from which data is read\n- InputFieldName (Text): Source field API name on the input object\n- OutputObjectName (Text): Target object API name to which data is written\n- OutputFieldName (Text): Target field API name on the output object\n- TransformValuesMappings (LongTextArea): JSON defining value transformations such as picklist mappings or formula expressions\n- FilterGroup (Number): Numeric group identifier for combining multiple filter conditions with AND/OR logic\n- FilterOperator (Text): Comparison operator for filtering such as equals, contains, or greater than\n- FilterValue (Text): The value to compare against when this item acts as a filter condition\n- DomainObjectFieldType (Text): Salesforce field type of the target field for proper type casting during load operations\n- MappingOrder (Number): Execution sequence determining the order in which mapping items are processed\n### OmniElectronicSignatureTemplate (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name identifying this signature template\n- TemplateId (Text): External DocuSign template identifier linking to the signing configuration\n- EnvelopeDocuments (LongTextArea): JSON defining the documents included in the signing envelope\n- RecipientSettings (LongTextArea): JSON configuring signer roles, routing order, and authentication requirements\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this template is available for use in OmniScript flows\n- Description (TextArea): Human-readable explanation of the signing scenario this template supports\n### OmniInteractionConfig (5 fields)\n- Name (Text): Record name for identifying this configuration entry. Often matches the Label field. Common values include ApexClassCheck, DocuSignAccountId, DocuSignNamedCredential, EnableLightningRTE, SkipUserProfileOnOSLoad, RetainDesignerSettingOnUpgrade, EnforceDMFLSAndDataEncryption, EnableQueryWithFLS\n- Key (Text): Configuration key used by the runtime engine to look up this setting. Auto-populated from the Name field. Used internally by OmniStudio components to query applicable configuration at execution time\n- Value (LongTextArea): The configuration value. For boolean toggles like ApexClassCheck, the value is the string true or false. For DocuSignAccountId, the value is the API Account ID from the DocuSign Apps and Keys page. For DocuSignNamedCredential, the value is the Salesforce Named Credential name (typically DocuSign)\n- Label (Text): Human-readable label displayed in the Omni Interaction Configuration list view in Setup. Typically matches the Name field. This is the value administrators see when browsing existing configuration records\n- Description (TextArea): Optional explanation of what this configuration controls and its valid values. Useful for documenting the purpose and expected behavior of each setting for other administrators\n### OmniAnalyticsTrackingDef (6 fields)\n- Name (Text): Name identifying this tracking event definition\n- TrackingGroup (Text): Logical grouping for organizing related tracking events in analytics views\n- EventType (Text): The type of user interaction tracked such as click, submit, navigation, or error\n- ComponentType (Picklist): The OmniStudio component type being tracked: OmniScript, FlexCard, or Integration Procedure\n- IsActive (Checkbox): Controls whether this tracking definition is currently collecting data\n- TrackingConfiguration (LongTextArea): JSON defining detailed tracking rules, filters, and data capture specifications\n### OmniDataPack (7 fields)\n- Name (Text): Display name identifying this DataPack bundle\n- Type (Picklist): The primary component type contained in this pack such as OmniScript, FlexCard, or Data Mapper\n- Status (Picklist): Deployment status such as Ready, Deployed, Error, or Superseded\n- DataPackData (LongTextArea): Serialized JSON containing the complete component definitions and dependency graph\n- Description (TextArea): Human-readable summary of what components this DataPack contains\n- SourceOrg (Text): Org identifier from which this DataPack was originally exported\n- Version (Text): Version label for tracking iterations of the same component bundle\n\n## Object Relationships\n- OmniProcess -> OmniProcessElement (parent): An OmniProcess contains one or more elements that define its steps, inputs, and actions\n- OmniProcess -> OmniProcessCompilation (parent): Active processes have compiled bundles stored for optimized runtime execution\n- OmniProcess -> OmniProcessTransientData (parent): Long-running processes can store temporary data between sessions\n- OmniProcess -> OmniscriptSavedSession (parent): Users can save in-progress OmniScript sessions for later resumption\n- OmniProcessElement -> OmniProcess (child): Each element belongs to a single OmniProcess parent record\n- OmniProcessElement -> OmniProcessElement (lookup): Elements can reference a parent element for hierarchical nesting within steps and blocks\n- OmniProcessCompilation -> OmniProcess (child): Each compilation record is generated from a single OmniProcess version\n- OmniProcessTransientData -> OmniProcess (child): Each transient data record belongs to a single OmniProcess\n- OmniscriptSavedSession -> OmniProcess (child): Each saved session belongs to a single OmniScript process\n- OmniUiCard -> OmniUiCard (lookup): FlexCards can embed child FlexCards via ChildCardKeys for composable interfaces\n- OmniUiCard -> OmniProcess (lookup): FlexCards can invoke OmniScripts as actions or use Integration Procedures as data sources\n- OmniDataTransformation -> OmniDataTransformationItem (parent): A Data Mapper contains one or more items defining individual field mappings and formulas\n- OmniDataTransformation -> OmniProcess (lookup): Data Mappers are invoked from Integration Procedures and OmniScripts as action elements\n- OmniDataTransformationItem -> OmniDataTransformation (child): Each mapping item belongs to a single parent Data Mapper definition\n- OmniElectronicSignatureTemplate -> OmniProcess (lookup): OmniScript processes reference signature templates through e-signature action elements\n- OmniInteractionConfig -> OmniProcess (lookup): Configuration settings affect the runtime behavior of all OmniScript and IP executions\n- OmniAnalyticsTrackingDef -> OmniProcess (lookup): Tracking definitions monitor user interactions within specific OmniScript processes\n- OmniDataPack -> OmniProcess (lookup): DataPacks reference OmniScript and Integration Procedure definitions they contain\n- OmniDataPack -> OmniUiCard (lookup): DataPacks reference FlexCard definitions included in the bundle\n- OmniDataPack -> OmniDataTransformation (lookup): DataPacks reference Data Mapper definitions included in the bundle\n\n## Cross-Domain Connections\n- OmniScripts <-> FlexCards: OmniScripts embed FlexCards for data display\n- OmniScripts <-> Integration Procedures: OmniScripts invoke Integration Procedures for server logic\n- OmniScripts <-> Data Mappers: OmniScripts call Data Mappers for data read/write\n- OmniScripts <-> Functions & Formulas: OmniScripts use formula functions in calculations\n- OmniScripts <-> OmniStudio Analytics: OmniScript interactions are tracked for analytics\n- OmniScripts <-> Testing & Quality: OmniScripts are validated in preview and test tools\n- OmniScripts <-> Agentforce Integration: OmniScripts are exposed as Agentforce actions\n- OmniScripts <-> Experience Cloud: OmniScripts are embedded in Experience Cloud pages\n- OmniScripts <-> Configuration & Settings: OmniScripts read interaction configuration settings\n- OmniScripts <-> Deployment & Migration: OmniScripts are exported via DataPacks\n- OmniScripts <-> Custom LWC Extensions: OmniScripts host custom LWC elements\n- FlexCards <-> Integration Procedures: FlexCards use Integration Procedures as data sources\n- FlexCards <-> Data Mappers: FlexCards read data through Data Mapper queries\n- FlexCards <-> Testing & Quality: FlexCards are tested with preview and UTAM framework\n- FlexCards <-> Agentforce Integration: FlexCards serve as context surfaces for agent actions\n- FlexCards <-> Experience Cloud: FlexCards are placed on Experience Cloud pages\n- FlexCards <-> Deployment & Migration: FlexCards are exported via DataPacks\n- FlexCards <-> Custom LWC Extensions: FlexCards host custom LWC components\n- Integration Procedures <-> Data Mappers: Integration Procedures invoke Data Mapper operations\n- Integration Procedures <-> Functions & Formulas: Integration Procedures use formula expressions in decision logic\n- Integration Procedures <-> Configuration & Settings: Integration Procedures respect interaction configuration\n- Integration Procedures <-> Deployment & Migration: Integration Procedures are exported via DataPacks\n- Data Mappers <-> Functions & Formulas: Data Mapper mappings use formula expressions\n- Data Mappers <-> Deployment & Migration: Data Mappers are exported via DataPacks\n- Configuration & Settings <-> OmniStudio Analytics: Usage entitlements feed analytics reporting\n- Configuration & Settings <-> Testing & Quality: Configuration settings affect test execution context\n- Configuration & Settings <-> Experience Cloud: Configuration defines Experience Cloud-specific behavior\n\n## Data Flows\n### OmniScripts\n1. Create an Omniscript with a unique Type, SubType, and Language combination.\n2. Arrange Steps, groups, inputs, formulas, Flexcards, and custom LWC elements on the designer canvas.\n3. Read applicable Omni Interaction Configuration settings and invoke Data Mappers or Integration Procedures as the process runs.\n4. Validate appearance, Data JSON, actions, and responses with Preview, Action Debugger, and test tools.\n5. Activate the Omniscript and place it on supported Lightning or Experience Cloud pages.\n6. Track interactions with OmniAnalytics, configure invoked Integration Procedures separately as Agentforce actions when needed, and move the Omniscript through Salesforce CLI or DataPacks as supported.\n### FlexCards\n1. Configure Flexcard data retrieval through Integration Procedures, Data Mapper queries, or other supported sources.\n2. Compose the layout, conditional states, child cards, and any hosted custom Lightning Web Components.\n3. Add actions that launch Omniscripts, open flyouts, navigate, fire events, update values, or support agent actions.\n4. Preview and debug the Flexcard, then automate applicable end-to-end checks with UTAM.\n5. Activate the Flexcard and place it on Lightning or Experience Cloud pages, or embed it in an Omniscript as a display element.\n6. Export supported Flexcards through DataPacks for deployment; use Salesforce CLI where the standard designer workflow requires it.\n### Integration Procedures\n1. Define the Integration Procedure inputs, outputs, actions, and security settings in IP Designer.\n2. Invoke Data Mapper operations, HTTP endpoints, Apex methods, and rules-based evaluations as required.\n3. Apply formula expressions, conditional paths, loops, caching, and error handling to control execution.\n4. Test the procedure, validate its responses, and activate the intended version.\n5. Call the Integration Procedure as Omniscript backend logic or use it as a Flexcard data source.\n6. Export the Integration Procedure through DataPacks for deployment between environments.\n### Data Mappers\n1. Define the Data Mapper type, source objects or input structure, filters, mappings, and formula expressions.\n2. Preview and test the Data Mapper with representative input and verify its output structure.\n3. Activate the Data Mapper before activating Flexcards or other components that reference it.\n4. Invoke the Data Mapper from an Omniscript or an Integration Procedure, or configure it as a Flexcard data source.\n5. Return extracted or transformed data to the caller, or persist mapped values through a Load operation.\n6. Export Data Mappers through DataPacks where supported, or use Salesforce CLI for standard-runtime deployment.\n### Functions & Formulas\n1. Supply formula inputs from the current OmniStudio component or its data.\n2. Apply conversions and string, date, mathematical, list, or JSON functions as needed.\n3. Compute derived values in Data Mapper mappings.\n4. Evaluate conditional logic in Integration Procedures.\n5. Use calculated results in Omniscript elements.\n### Custom LWC Extensions\n1. Identify an Omniscript element or Flexcard rendering requirement not covered declaratively.\n2. Set up and implement the custom Lightning Web Component.\n3. Connect the component to the Omniscript data context or Flexcard context and events.\n4. Add the component to the applicable Omniscript or Flexcard composition.\n5. Preview and test the complete user experience.\n6. Deploy the Lightning Web Component and related OmniStudio content.\n### Configuration & Settings\n1. Create Omni Interaction Configuration records for runtime, security, Integration Procedure, and Experience Cloud behavior.\n2. Configure Omni Global Auto Number definitions and invoke GLOBALAUTONUMBER from Data Mappers or Integration Procedures.\n3. Connect DocuSign, fetch templates, map Omniscript data, and add signature or envelope actions.\n4. Run Omniscripts and Integration Procedures under the configured org settings and security checks.\n5. Preview and test OmniStudio solutions in the resulting runtime, locale, user, and deployment context.\n6. Monitor parent Omniscript call consumption in Company Information under Usage-based Entitlements.\n### Experience Cloud\n1. Activate the Omniscripts and Flexcards intended for the Experience Cloud site.\n2. Configure Omni Interaction Configuration security checks for the portal runtime.\n3. Grant guest or authenticated users the required permissions and record access.\n4. Place Omniscripts and Flexcards on the appropriate Experience Builder pages.\n5. Configure custom LWC wrappers or nested dependencies when the site runtime requires them.\n6. Preview, publish, and test the site under each intended user context.\n### Agentforce Integration\n1. Enable OmniStudio metadata support and assign the required OmniStudio permissions.\n2. Select the OmniScript or Integration Procedure to expose as an Agentforce action.\n3. Define and verify Integration Procedure input and output JSON, then save and activate it.\n4. Add the action from the Agentforce Studio Asset Library to the appropriate subagent.\n5. Configure the agent and subagent for the intended conversation task.\n6. Invoke the action during an interaction and present relevant context through Flexcards.\n### OmniStudio Analytics\n1. Enable Omnistudio Analytics and select the required event storage settings.\n2. Create Tracking Groups and add active Omniscripts and Flexcards to track.\n3. Add the tracked components to Lightning or Experience Cloud pages with Enable Omnistudio Analytics selected.\n4. Route interaction events through tracking components to internal storage, Google Analytics 4, or both.\n5. Review engagement reports in the Tracking Group, and monitor OmniStudio call consumption separately under Usage-based Entitlements.\n### Testing & Quality\n1. Open the Omniscript or Flexcard configuration in its OmniStudio designer.\n2. Review Design Assistant alerts and refine the component against reported recommendations.\n3. Preview the component with representative data and the applicable interaction configuration context.\n4. Exercise guided paths, conditional behavior, displayed data, and configured actions.\n5. Run UTAM end-to-end tests for the Omniscript or Flexcard before deployment.\n### Deployment & Migration\n1. Create or update OmniStudio components in a development org.\n2. Create new component versions where editable variants are required.\n3. Package Omniscript, Flexcard, Integration Procedure, and Data Mapper definitions for deployment.\n4. Separate setup metadata from non-setup OmniStudio entities.\n5. Deploy the components to a test or integration org and validate them.\n6. Deploy the validated components to the production org.\n\n## Links\nUse these URLs when mentioning domains or components:\n- [OmniScripts](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/omniscripts)\n- [FlexCards](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/flexcards)\n- [Integration Procedures](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/integration)\n- [Data Mappers](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/datamappers)\n- [Functions & Formulas](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/formulas)\n- [Custom LWC Extensions](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/lwc)\n- [Configuration & Settings](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/config)\n- [Experience Cloud](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/experience)\n- [Agentforce Integration](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/agentforce)\n- [OmniStudio Analytics](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/analytics)\n- [Testing & Quality](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/testing)\n- [Deployment & Migration](https://epugach.github.io/product-explorer/omnistudio/#/deployment)\n";
